"""The Necrobinder's strategy, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: card removals and a smaller deck, Block and preserving Osty's HP, while
looking for scaling.
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "necrobinder-strategic-planner-prompt-1"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S DECK. Keep the deck small, once it can win act 1. The fight runs through a few cards and one companion: Osty is summoned again every turn by the starting relic, the Summon cards raise his HP, and his attacks hit harder the more HP he has. Every card added delays those cards. In act 1 the deck still needs its first Block, damage and Summon cards, and a deck held small from the first picks is too weak for the act 1 boss (in simulated runs it lost that fight more often). From act 2 the deck_balance track "deck_size" holds the deck to a target size: at or above it a card is added only when it fills a missing or short role or track or adds scaling the deck is not yet full of, and skipping is the default for everything else. Removals come before additions in every act: at a shop buy the removal before any card, prefer events and relics that remove or transform, and remove starter Strikes before starter Defends, because this deck's damage comes from Osty and from Doom while its plain Block is thin. A curse or status that can be removed goes first.

Block and Osty's HP are one defensive budget. While Osty is alive he absorbs all unblocked attack damage, so his HP is Block that lasts between turns, and it is also his damage. Keep him alive: take Block and Summon cards (the track "summon" counts the cards that Summon, the starter's included) until both are covered, and block the big attack turns yourself so they do not kill him. A card that kills Osty for a one-time effect (the track "osty") gives up the HP the Summon cards built and the attacks that use it; take one only with a named way to get the fight finished by it, and never a second.

With the deck small and defended, look for scaling, because flat damage does not end act 2 and act 3 bosses: Doom applied every turn or by a Power (an enemy dies at the end of its turn once its Doom reaches its HP), Osty's damage growing through his HP or a Power, an attack that grows permanently, or a Soul or Ethereal payoff whose other half is already held. One route that a small deck draws every cycle beats two half-built ones: once a route is covered, the first card of another is a skip."""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S ELITE ROUTES. The Necrobinder has the lowest HP of the five and no healing after fights, so HP spent on the route is not coming back before the next rest site, and elites are where it goes: in simulated A10 runs a strong drafter lost about one elite in six in act 1 and one in three in act 2 with this character, against one in twenty-five and one in eleven with the Ironclad, and an elite it beat cost a third of max HP or more. So elites are taken selectively in every act, and act 1 can plan none when the deck, HP or recovery cannot support one. Take act 1 elites when Osty's HP and the deck's Block can absorb the elite's big turns: a route with a rest site before or right behind the elite, and Block or Summon picked up in the rooms before it. An elite with several enemies or summons needs an attack that hits ALL enemies or Doom on ALL enemies first. Prefer routes that pass a shop, because the removal is this deck's best purchase, and count it when comparing a shop route with an extra elite. In later acts plan at most one elite, behind a rest site, and none while Block scaling or damage scaling is still missing; a second only with HP to spare and a rest site between them. State which elites are accepted, where the shop and rest sites fall, and what would remove or add one."""
